{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 20, :comments ["<b>But for Adam he did not find a helpmate opposite him.</b> This is the language of Rashi: when He brought them before him, He brought them male and female. He said, every one has a mate, but I have no mate. Immediately, \"And YHWH Elohim caused a deep sleep to fall upon him.\" And his explanation is fine, for since he inserted the verses of the naming among the matter of the helpmate, this will prove it. And the naming, according to the opinion of the commentators, is as its simple sense — that each one should have its own name, so that they would be known and recognized by its descendants through the names that he would call them, for its name shall endure forever. Now the Holy One, blessed be He, when He desired to make for him a helpmate, then He brought them before him, for it was necessary to bring them to him in pairs so that he would call a name also for their females — for some of them he called by one name, and some are distinct, such as ox and cow, he-goat and she-goat, ram and ewe, and others besides. And when he saw them mated one with another he desired them, and when he did not find among them a helpmate for himself he was distressed and slept, and Elohim caused a deep sleep to fall upon him so that he would not feel the removal of a rib from his body.\n\nIn my opinion, however, the naming is itself the helpmate. The matter is as follows: the Holy One, blessed be He, brought every beast of the field and every bird of the heavens before Adam, and he recognized their nature and called them names, that is, the name appropriate to them according to their nature, and through the names the appropriate matter of one being a helpmate to the other was made clear — that is, those fit to reproduce, this one from that one. And even if we hold concerning the names that they are by convention and not natural, we may say that the calling of the names is the distinguishing of the species, for they passed before him male and female, and he considered their nature — which of them is a helpmate to the other, that is, which reproduces from it — and he made this known through the names. For the small cattle he called by one name, since all of them are a helpmate one to the other in reproduction, that they reproduce this one from that one; and the large cattle by another name, and the wild beast by another name, since they do not reproduce this species from that one, and so with all of them. And he did not find among all of them that it should be in its nature a helpmate for him and be called by his name, for the calling of names is the distinguishing of the species and the separation of their powers one from another, as I explained above.\n\nThe meaning is not that it was in Adam's hand to find among them a helpmate for himself, for they were created in their nature; rather, if he had seen his nature suited to one of the species and chosen it, the Holy One, blessed be He, would have adapted its nature to him, as He did with the rib, and He would not have needed to fashion it with a new construction. And this is the meaning of \"whatsoever the man would call every living creature, that is its name\" — that is, it shall be its name: the Holy One, blessed be He, would fulfill that name upon it in accordance with the matter I have explained.\n\nAnd the correct view in my eyes is that it was not the desire before Him, blessed be He, to take his rib from him until Adam knew that among the created beings there was no helpmate for him, and that he would desire to have a helpmate like them; and because of this it was necessary to take from him one of his ribs. And this is the meaning of \"but for Adam he did not find a helpmate opposite him\" — that is, for Adam's name there was not found a helpmate that would be fitting opposite him and be called by his name, reproducing from him. And there is no need here for the words of the commentators, who said that a proper name comes here in place of a pronoun — \"and for him he did not find a helpmate opposite him\" — in the manner of \"Lemekh's wives\" (Genesis 4:23), \"and Yiftah and Shemuel\" (I Samuel 12:11).\n\nAnd this is what he said: \"This time\" — that is, this time I have found a helpmate for myself, which I did not find until now among the other species, for she is bone of my bones and flesh of my flesh, and it is fitting that she be called by my name literally, for we shall reproduce this one from that one.\n\nAnd in the word \"this\" (זֹאת) there is a secret; it will be understood from our words in the portion of וְזֹאת הַבְּרָכָה, if my Rock will grant me to reach there. And therefore he reversed it: \"for from a man was this one taken.\" Understand."], :book :genesis, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 2, :notes {0 "Ramban cites Ibn Ezra and Radak on v. 18, and references Maimonides' Guide for the Perplexed II:30 and Pirkei DeRabbi Eliezer ch. 12. The final kabbalistic hint about זֹאת cross-references his commentary on Deuteronomy 33, which he expresses uncertainty about reaching — possibly an autobiographical allusion."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T04:20:56.089371710Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}